<h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>Plain wrapping paper is not toxic, but VCA says it "can cause an intestinal obstruction, especially if it is made of foil or other indigestible material."</li>
<li>Ribbon and string are the bigger danger: a linear foreign body hazard, "especially for puppies," that can tangle in the intestines and require surgery (VCA).</li>
<li>Never pull a ribbon or string hanging from the mouth or bottom; an anchored linear foreign body can perforate the gut if pulled (Merck).</li>
<li>Cats are more prone than dogs to linear foreign body obstructions from string-like material, so treat a cat that ate ribbon as more urgent.</li>
<li>Repeated vomiting or a painful belly need an emergency vet; not eating or straining to pass stool need a vet the same day.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Is Wrapping Paper Toxic to Dogs?</h2>
<p>No. Plain paper is generally not toxic to dogs; the main danger is physical, not chemical. A dog cannot digest paper, so a large wad can sit in the stomach or catch in the intestines instead of passing through. VCA Animal Hospitals' holiday hazard guide for dog owners says "wrapping paper can cause an intestinal obstruction, especially if it is made of foil or other indigestible material," and the same logic extends to glossy or glitter-coated sheets, since the coating is just as indigestible as foil.</p>
<p>Most dogs that eat a small torn piece pass it without a problem, sometimes with mild vomiting or a soft stool. A larger amount or foil-coated paper can bunch up and block the gut. You cannot tell which outcome your dog will have, which is why the amount eaten and paper type matter when you call your vet.</p>

<h2>Is Tissue Paper Dangerous for Dogs?</h2>
<p>Usually not in small amounts. Tissue paper is thinner and softer than wrapping paper, so it is less likely to cause a problem than a wad of stiff gift wrap, but a dog that shredded and swallowed a lot of it still needs a call to the vet. Puppies are at higher risk with any of this: VCA flags ribbons and strings used to wrap gifts as hazardous "especially to young puppies," and a puppy is more likely to swallow a large piece relative to its smaller gut. Our <a href="/tools/dog-first-aid-guide/">dog first aid guide</a> covers what to have ready when you call, and our <a href="/blog/new-puppy-checklist-everything-you-need/">new puppy checklist</a> covers chew toys and gating off the gift pile.</p>

<h2>Dog Ate Ribbon or String: The Bigger Risk</h2>
<p>If your dog ate ribbon, string, curling ribbon or yarn with the wrapping paper, treat it more seriously than plain paper alone. These are classic linear foreign bodies, the same category of risk covered in our <a href="/blog/dog-ate-tinsel/">dog ate tinsel guide</a>: a long, thin object that anchors at one end, usually near the base of the tongue or in the stomach, while the rest moves into the intestines. The Merck Veterinary Manual explains that as the gut tries to push the object along, it bunches up and the material can cut through the intestinal wall, a higher perforation risk than other swallowed objects. VCA's dog and cat holiday guides both call ribbon and string dangerous "especially for puppies" and "especially for kittens."</p>
<p>If ribbon or string is hanging from your dog's mouth or bottom, do not pull it, and call your vet. Merck warns that pulling an anchored linear foreign body can perforate the gut if it is embedded in tissue; a vet needs to check whether the other end is still caught first.</p>

<h2>Cellophane, Tape, and How Much Was Eaten</h2>
<p>Cellophane and plastic gift wrap are not toxic, but indigestible, and can bunch up the same way foil-coated paper does; a small scrap is usually not worrying, but a larger piece deserves a call to your vet. Scotch tape and duct tape are also not toxic; a long strip or sticky wad can add to a blockage risk, especially swallowed with a lot of paper or ribbon.</p>
<p>The amount eaten matters more than the material. A small torn corner is a different situation from a dog that got into an entire roll or a pile of paper and ribbon after gifts were opened, since a large wad is far more likely to get stuck. When in doubt, call your vet rather than guess.</p>

<h2>When Does Paper Become an Emergency?</h2>
<p>Regardless of amount, choking or gagging while swallowing, or ribbon or string visible at the mouth or bottom, are emergencies on their own. Otherwise, paper becomes an emergency once any blockage sign below appears. A dog acting normal a few hours later is reassuring but not a guarantee, so keep watching for a day or two.</p>

<h3>How long does it take a dog to pass wrapping paper?</h3>
<p>There is no reliable fixed timeframe. Merck's general guidance on monitored foreign bodies treats roughly 36 to 48 hours without passage as a point to re-check with imaging, but that applies to small, smooth objects, not bunched paper or anchored ribbon. Eating, drinking and normal stool without vomiting is reassuring; your vet can advise on watching versus bringing your dog in.</p>

