<h2>What Will the Vet Do?</h2>
<p>A vet typically starts with a physical exam, checking the mouth for an anchored strand, then imaging such as X-rays or ultrasound plus bloodwork (Cornell). For a small amount and a dog that looks well, monitoring or serial X-rays may confirm the material is moving through. Endoscopy can retrieve objects in the stomach (Merck), but is often not suitable for linear foreign bodies like ribbon. Cornell notes many obstructions need surgery to remove the material and, if the bowel is damaged, to remove and reconnect that section. Our <a href="/tools/pet-stomach-upset-guide/">pet stomach upset guide</a> covers digestive upset, but treat ongoing vomiting after wrapping paper as a possible blockage until your vet says otherwise.</p>

<h2>Cat Ate Wrapping Paper? Cats Have Their Own Risks</h2>
<p>If your cat ate wrapping paper, cellophane or tissue paper, small amounts of plain paper are usually not an emergency, but watch for vomiting, not eating or lethargy. Cat ate ribbon or string deserves faster action: Merck states cats are more prone than dogs to a linear foreign body obstruction, usually from playing with string-like material, and VCA's cat-owner holiday guide calls ribbon and string a hazard "especially for kittens" that can cut through the intestinal wall. Never pull ribbon or string visible at a cat's rear end; see a vet instead. Our <a href="/tools/cat-vomiting-guide/">cat vomiting guide</a> covers other causes of vomiting, and if your cat climbs decorations too, read <a href="/blog/how-to-cat-proof-a-christmas-tree/">how to cat-proof a Christmas tree</a>.</p>

<h2>Other Holiday Wrapping Hazards</h2>
<p>Gift-opening day brings several risks together: bows chewed into stringy pieces (treat like ribbon), candy eaten still in its wrapper (see our <a href="/blog/thanksgiving-foods-for-dogs-and-cats/">guide to foods unsafe for dogs and cats</a>), and batteries in the debris, covered by our <a href="/tools/pet-poison-emergency-guide/">pet poison emergency guide</a>. Our <a href="/tools/holiday-pet-safety-guide/">holiday pet safety guide</a> rounds up more seasonal hazards.</p>

<h2>How Do I Stop My Dog Eating Wrapping Paper?</h2>
<p>Dogs often go for wrapping paper out of curiosity, boredom, or the crinkly texture and food smells clinging to it, rather than hunger. A dog that regularly swallows non-food items, sometimes called pica, may benefit from more enrichment and a vet visit to rule out a cause. The easiest fix is to clean up as you go rather than leaving paper, ribbon and bows in a pile.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Bag it immediately</strong> as gifts are opened, rather than leaving a pile on the floor.</li>
<li><strong>Keep gifts off the ground</strong> beforehand, since a wrapped gift that smells like food can tempt a dog to tear in.</li>
<li><strong>Supervise gift-opening</strong> and give your dog a toy or chew of their own instead.</li>
<li><strong>Take trash out promptly</strong> rather than leaving a bag of debris somewhere a dog can tip over.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/tools/pet-proof-home-guide/">room-by-room pet-proofing guide</a> has more ways to make the house safer year-round.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Is wrapping paper toxic to dogs?</h3>
<p>No. The risk is physical rather than chemical: a dog cannot digest paper, so a large amount or foil-coated paper can cause an intestinal obstruction, which VCA names as a specific hazard of wrapping paper.</p>

<h3>Will tissue paper hurt my dog?</h3>
<p>Usually not in small amounts. Tissue paper carries the same type of risk as wrapping paper but is thinner and softer, so a small amount is less likely to cause trouble than stiffer gift wrap. A dog that swallowed a large wad, or a small dog that ate a lot of it, should still be checked by a vet, since the underlying blockage risk is the same.</p>

<h3>What if my dog ate a plastic wrapper or cellophane?</h3>
<p>Cellophane and plastic wrap are not toxic, but according to VCA's logic on indigestible wrapping materials, they can bunch up inside the gut much like foil-coated paper. A small scrap is usually not a concern; call your vet for a larger piece or if any blockage signs appear.</p>

<h3>Is foil-coated wrapping paper worse than plain paper?</h3>
<p>It is not more toxic, but it is harder for a dog's gut to break down. VCA Animal Hospitals names foil or other indigestible material as a factor that raises the risk of an intestinal obstruction, so treat foil, glitter and glossy-coated paper more cautiously than plain paper.</p>

<h3>Should I worry about a small amount vs. a whole roll?</h3>
<p>Yes. A small torn corner is a much lower risk than a whole roll or a pile of paper and ribbon from gift-opening day. The more that was eaten, the more likely it is to sit in the stomach or get stuck in the intestines, so call your vet when the amount is more than a scrap.</p>

<h3>What if my dog ate wrapping paper along with candy or a gift inside it?</h3>
<p>Treat it as two separate questions: the paper is a possible blockage risk, and anything eaten with it, such as chocolate, gum with xylitol, or medication, is a separate poisoning risk. Tell your vet or a poison helpline exactly what was inside the gift, since that can change the advice you get.</p>

<h3>Why does my dog keep eating wrapping paper?</h3>
<p>Often curiosity, boredom, or the crinkly texture and lingering food smells, rather than hunger. Dogs that repeatedly swallow non-food items may be showing a behavior called pica, which is worth mentioning to your vet, especially if it happens outside the holidays too.</p>

<h3>Should I make my dog throw up after eating wrapping paper?</h3>
<p>No, not on your own. A bulky or stringy object can cause harm coming back up. Call your vet or a poison helpline first; they will tell you whether inducing vomiting is appropriate for what your dog ate.</p>

